Change detection:


- The second part of the problem is the change detection itself. It’s one thing to know when and how
    it’s started, but it’s another to know how it works.

- 🔥First of all, we have to remember that an Angular application is a tree of components. When the
    change detection runs, it starts walking through the components of the tree, starting from the root
    component and going down to the leaves.

- 🔥🧠When checking a component, Angular evaluates all the expressions used in the template, and
    compares each result with the previous one. If an evaluation result is not the same, then the DOM
    is updated. The expression that has a new value could also be used as the input of a child
    component. In that case, Angular updates the input of the child component and calls its
    ngOnChanges hook, which might change the state of the child component. Then the child component
    is checked.

- 💡💡Before Angular 22, the default change detection strategy of components was Default, now renamed
    to Eager. So, unless components explicitly opted for the OnPush strategy, every expression of
    every component of the tree was checked during each change detection.

- 💡The tree traversal, fortunately for performance, is done only once per change detection. But this
    comes with another disadvantage: ⚠️you can introduce a bug if, during the change detection of a
    child component, you update its own state or the state of a parent. ⚠️Since Angular does a single
    pass, this change will go unnoticed until the next time ZoneJS triggers a new change detection.

- 😮Angular forbids you from doing that. During development, each change detection actually does two
    traversals. If the second traversal detects a change, then Angular throws an error to warn you that
    you broke the unidirectional data flow principle (the infamous
    ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError).

- ⚠️As you can see, change detection is quite brute-force. The expressions in your templates are
    evaluated many many times. This is why you should make sure they don’t invoke complex, slow
    functions.

- 🔥🔥Since Angular 22, the default change detection strategy of components is OnPush. Modern
    applications using this default for all their components are more efficient, because change
    detection can skip most components during the tree traversal.


